      "text": "Given that when an integer \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7, we can express \\( n \\) as:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe are asked to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. First, let's consider the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3. Since \\( n = 15k + 7 \\), where 15 is divisible by 3, we can write:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\nTo simplify \\( 7 \\pmod{3} \\), we find the remainder when 7 is divided by 3:\n\\[ 7 \\div 3 = 2 \\text{ remainder } 1 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, let's consider the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. Similarly, we can write:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nand since 5 is not a factor of 15, the remainder when \\( 15k \\) is divided by 5 must be zero:\n\\[ 15k \\equiv 0 \\pmod{5} \\]\nTherefore, we only need to consider the remainder of 7 when divided by 5, which is:\n\\[ 7 \\div 5 = 1 \\text{ remainder } 2 \\]\nSo,\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nFinally, we need to find the sum of these remainders:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the remainders when the same integer \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 is:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
