      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "Let's denote the integer as \\( n \\). According to the problem, when \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7. This can be expressed mathematically as:\n\\[ n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\]\n\nWe need to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5, and then sum these remainders.\n\n### Finding the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3\n\nSince \\( n \\equiv 7 \\pmod{15} \\), \\( n \\) can be written in the form:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\). We need to find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3. Let's substitute \\( n \\) in the division by 3:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nDividing both sides by 3:\n\\[ \\frac{15k + 7}{3} = 5k + \\frac{7}{3} \\]\nThe term \\( \\frac{7}{3} \\) represents the remainder. When we divide 7 by 3, the quotient is 2 and the remainder is:\n\\[ 7 = 2 \\times 3 + 1 \\]\nThus, \\( 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\). So, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\n### Finding the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5\n\nSimilarly, we can express \\( n \\) in the form:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nWe need to find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5. Substituting \\( n \\) in the division by 5:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nDividing both sides by 5:\n\\[ \\frac{15k + 7}{5} = 3k + \\frac{7}{5} \\]\nThe term \\( \\frac{7}{5} \\) represents the remainder. When we divide 7 by 5, the quotient is 1 and the remainder is:\n\\[ 7 = 2 \\times 5 + 2 \\]\nThus, \\( 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\). So, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\n### Summing the remainders\n\nThe remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1, and the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2. Therefore, the sum of the remainders is:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nConclusion:\n\\[ \\boxed{3} \\]",
