      "text": "Given that when an integer \\( n \\) is divided by 15, the remainder is 7, we can express this situation as:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\]\nfor some integer \\( k \\).\n\nWe are asked to find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5. First, let's find the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3. We can rewrite \\( n \\) in modulo 3 terms:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{3} \\]\nSince \\( 15k \\) is a multiple of 3, it contributes nothing to the remainder when divided by 3, so we only need to consider \\( 7 \\pmod{3} \\):\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 1 \\pmod{3} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 3 is 1.\n\nNext, let's find the remainders when \\( n \\) is divided by 5:\n\\[ n = 15k + 7 \\equiv 7 \\pmod{5} \\]\nAgain, we only need to consider \\( 7 \\pmod{5} \\):\n\\[ 7 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{5} \\]\nThus, the remainder when \\( n \\) is divided by 5 is 2.\n\nFinally, we need to find the sum of these remainders:\n\\[ 1 + 2 = 3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the remainders when the integer \\( n \\) is divided by 3 and by 5 is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
